Construct Validity of a New Outcome Measure
Assessing Self-Reported Impairments in People with
Post-Polio Syndrome.
Jan Lexell, MD PhD (Skane University Hospital, Lund,
Sweden); Christina Brogårdh, PhD, RPT.
Disclosures: J. Lexell, No Disclosures.
Objective: To evaluate the construct validity of a new outcome
measure that assesses self-reported impairments in persons with
post-polio syndrome (PPS).
Design: Psychometric analysis of data on self-perceived impair-
ments in PPS.
Setting: Specialized post-polio clinic in a university hospital out-
patient department.
Participants: Two hundred seventy three persons with prior po-
lio (119 men and 154 women, mean age 63.5 years).
Interventions: Not applicable.
Main Outcome Measures: Rasch analysis of a 13-item self-
reported outcome measure using 5 categories, where the partici-
pants had rated how much they have been bothered by various
PPS-related impairments during the past 2 weeks.
Results: The initial analysis showed disordered categories, misﬁt
with some of the items, multidimensionality and local dependency.
After adjustment of the categories resulting in a 4-category measure,
a ﬁt to the model was achieved, but it was still multi-dimensional.
Analyses of local dependency revealed correlations between some of
the items, which resulted in a ﬁve testlet solution. This solution gave
ﬁt to the model and uni-dimensionality.
Conclusions: This new outcome measure can be considered as
uni-dimensional after adjustment of the categories and local depen-
dency. The good psychometric properties makes it a useful outcome
measure that will increase our understanding of the impairments
people with PPS can experience and may assist clinicians in the
planning and evaluation of appropriate rehabilitation interventions.
